<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indonesian National Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as PNI, Partai Nasional Indonesia, Indonesian Nationalist Party</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indonesian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> (PNI) was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> and others in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung">Bandung</a> on 4 July 1927 to win independence through mass non-cooperation with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>. The colonial government arrested Sukarno in 1929, and the party dissolved itself in 1931. A new PNI founded after independence became one of the four largest parties in the 1955 elections.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indonesian-national-party.jpg" alt="Indonesian National Party" width="320" height="264" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kaliper1 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Partai_Nasional_Indonesia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party grew out of the Algemeene Studie Club that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a>, then an engineering student at the Bandung Technical College, founded in November 1925 on the model of a club Soetomo had started in Surabaya. Reformed in July 1927 as the Indonesian National Association, it took the name Indonesian National Party in May 1928. Its aim was economic and political independence for the archipelago, and by the end of 1929 it had 10,000 members.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> and seven other leaders were tried as a threat to public order and sentenced in September 1930 to one to three years, Sukarno receiving the longest term. Rival successors followed, among them Partindo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutan-sjahrir">Sutan Sjahrir</a>'s Indonesian Nationalist Education, the New PNI, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-hatta">Mohammad Hatta</a> led from 1932.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Two days after the declaration of independence in August 1945, a state party of the same name was set up as a continuation of the wartime Jawa Hokokai, but its ties to the Japanese occupation made it unpopular and it was dissolved within weeks. On 29 January 1946 the PNI was revived as a popular party without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a>, who as president stood above politics. During the National Revolution it opposed negotiating with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and drew support from the administrative officers of the republic.</p><p>The party took 22.3% of the vote and 57 of the 257 seats in the 1955 general election, ahead of Masyumi, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahdlatul-ulama">Nahdlatul Ulama</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-indonesia">Communist Party of Indonesia</a> (PKI). Its members Wilopo and Ali Sastroamidjojo headed cabinets in the 1950s. Under Guided Democracy it moved left, and in 1963 its congress defined its ideology of Marhaenism as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a> adapted to Indonesian conditions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the coup attempt of 30 September 1965 the army forced out the party's left wing. Weakened, the PNI came third in the 1971 elections with less than 7% of the vote, and in 1973 it was merged into the Indonesian Democratic Party. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> several parties claimed its name; in the 1999 election two of them won one seat each.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Indonesian National Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Western-style schools created a small educated elite, and the first modern organisations followed: Budi Utomo in 1908, Sarekat Islam, which grew into a mass movement in the 1910s, the Communist Party of the Indies in 1920 and Sukarno’s <b>Indonesian Nationalist Party</b> in 1927.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Organisations such as Budi Utomo (1908), Sarekat Islam and the <b>Indonesian National Party</b> of Sukarno gave the colony's many peoples a common political language, and at the Youth Pledge of 1928 delegates adopted one nation, one people and one language, Indonesian, a form of Malay.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
